= Property — Technical =

== RDF property extension ==
Under RDF semantics, a property denotes a binary relation whose extension is a set of ordered pairs.

If p denotes property P:

<pre>
IEXT(P) ⊆ IR × IR
</pre>

with datatype/literal details governed by the interpretation.

== Subproperty ==
RDFS semantics for:

<pre>
p1 rdfs:subPropertyOf p2
</pre>

requires:

<pre>
IEXT(p1) ⊆ IEXT(p2)
</pre>

This licenses entailment from a p1 assertion to a p2 assertion.

== Domain / range ==
RDFS domain:

<pre>
IEXT(p) subject → instance of C
</pre>

Range similarly types objects.

Multiple domain declarations are conjunctive under RDFS semantics, not alternatives.

== OWL object properties ==
OWL object property expressions support:
* inverses;
* property chains;
* functionality;
* inverse functionality;
* symmetry;
* asymmetry;
* transitivity;
* reflexivity;
* irreflexivity;
* disjointness.

These axioms can create non-obvious consequences.

== Data properties ==
OWL data properties map individuals to datatype values.

Datatype semantics differ from object-property semantics because literals denote values in datatype value spaces.

== Constraint versus inference ==
RDFS/OWL statements describe semantics.

SHACL-like shapes describe conformance constraints.

Using ontology axioms as if they were validation-only rules can infer types rather than report errors.

